In the Public Bill Office a committee proof of one clause of a bill has been pulled, and beside it lies the amendment slip that will be tabled against that clause the same morning. Both lie on the green baize of the committee table inside the pool of the table lamp, square on to the camera.

The clause is already cast off at this measure, and its make-up is settled:
  the clause heading 'Clause 2  Register of coastal moorings' occupies one line;
  subsection (1) runs to four lines;
  subsection (2) runs to three lines;
  subsection (3) runs to six lines;
  subsection (4) runs to two lines;
  subsection (5) runs to five lines.
Nothing else stands inside the text area, and no line is left blank between subsections.

The office rule this proof is pulled under: a small line number stands in the left margin against every fifth line of the clause, the clause heading counting as line one. Those margin figures are the only reason the House can debate the clause at all, because an amendment has to say which line it bites on.

Two amendments go on the slip beside the proof:
  Amendment 1  Clause 2, line ___ , leave out 'may' and insert 'must'  -- this one alters the opening line of subsection (4);
  Amendment 2  Clause 2, line ___ , at the beginning insert 'Before making regulations under this section,'  -- this one alters the opening line of subsection (5).
Each amendment must carry the line figure of the line it bites on, in place of the blank. Which lines those are is deliberately being kept from you: working them out from the make-up above is the whole of the office's job here.

No other numbering may stand on the proof.
